  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": ")Sh.Manoj Rana vs )State on 4 November, 2020 1 In The Court Of Sh.Dinesh Kumar Sharma Principal District & Sessions Judge/ Food Safety Appellate Tribunal New Delhi Fsat No.04/2020 1.)Sh.Manoj Rana S/o Sh.Dharam Pal Singh M/s (Cfl) Creamy Foods Ltd. 80th K.M.Stone, G.T.Road Village Bartoli Khurja, Bulandshahar203131. 2.)M/s (Cfl) Creamy Foods Ltd. 80th K.M.Stone, G.T.Road Village Bartoli Khurja, Bulandshahar203131. .....Appellants Vs 1.)State, through Food Safety Officer Department of Food Safety Govt. of Nct of Delhi 8th Floor, Mayur Bhawan Connaught Place, New Delhi110001. 2.)Sh.R.P.Singh Food Safety Officer Department of Food Safety Govt. of Nct of Delhi 8th Floor, Mayur Bhawan Connaught Place, New Delhi110001. .....Respondents Date of filing of appeal : 28.01.2020 Date of arguments : 04.11.2020 Date of judgment : 04.11.2020 2. The facts in brief are that on 31.01.2018 at about 4.30pm, Sh.Ram Pratap Singh, Food Safety Officer( \"FSO\" in short) alongwith Dr.P.M.Kothekar, Designated Officer and Sh.Subedar Mishra, the then Field Assistant visited M/s Singhal Enterprises, A64/2, East Nathu Colony, Mandoli Road, Shahdara, Delhi110093 for inspection.",
    "facts": "2. The facts in brief are that on 31.01.2018 at about 4.30pm, Sh.Ram Pratap Singh, Food Safety Officer( \"FSO\" in short) alongwith Dr.P.M.Kothekar, Designated Officer and Sh.Subedar Mishra, the then Field Assistant visited M/s Singhal Enterprises, A64/2, East Nathu Colony, Mandoli Road, Shahdara, Delhi110093 for inspection. After inspection of the said shop, a sample of \"Ghee\" for analysis was taken. The sample consisted of 4x1 litre of \"Ghee\" Madhusudan Brand (ready for sale) in original sealed condition having identical label declaration. Thereafter, the sample food article was divided into four equal parts and each counterpart of the sample was separately packed, fastened and sealed according to the provisions of The Food Safety and Standards Act 2006 ( \"FSS Act\" in short). Thereafter, one counterpart was sent to the Food Analyst for analysis on 01.02.2018. )Sh.Manoj Rana vs )State on 4 November, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/148970849/ 1 The remaining two counterparts of the sample in a sealed packet as well as fourth counterpart in a separate sealed packet were deposited with the Designated Officer. The Food Analyst opined vide his report no.FSS/93/2018 dated 12.02.2018 that \" The sample is misbranded because there is violation of Regulation No.2.3.2 of The Food Safety and Standards (Packaging and Labelling) Regulations, 2011 (\"FSS (P&L) Regulations\" in short)\". Thereafter, the FSO filed an application under Rule 3.1.1(3) of The Food Safety and Standards Rules,2011( \"FSS Rules\" in short) before Ld.A.O. on 27.06.2019.",
    "arguments": "3. Without going into the further merits of the case, Sh.Kapil Sharma, Ld.Counsel for the appellant has submitted that the present complaint is barred by limitation. It has been submitted that the sample in the present case was taken on 31.01.2018 and the complaint was filed on 27.06.2019 and there was no order of extension granted by The Commissioner of Food Safety. 4. Sh.Kundan Lal, Ld.Chief PP for respondent has submitted that there is no order of The Commissioner of Food Safety regarding extension of limitation. 5. Section 77 of FSS Act reads as under: \"77. Time limit for prosecutions. Notwithstanding anything contained in this Act, no court shall take cognizance of an offence under this Act after the expiry of the period of one year from the date of commission of an offence: Provided that the Commissioner of Food Safety may, for reasons to be recorded in writing, approve prosecution within an extended period of up to three years.\" 6.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Without going into the further merits of the case, Sh.Kapil Sharma, Ld.Counsel for the appellant has submitted that the present complaint is barred by limitation. It has been submitted that the sample in the present case was taken on 31.01.2018 and the complaint was filed on 27.06.2019 and there was no order of extension granted by The Commissioner of Food Safety. 4. Sh.Kundan Lal, Ld.Chief PP for respondent has submitted that there is no order of The Commissioner of Food Safety regarding extension of limitation.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "5. Section 77 of FSS Act reads as under: \"77. Time limit for prosecutions. Notwithstanding anything contained in this Act, no court shall take cognizance of an offence under this Act after the expiry of the period of one year from the date of commission of an offence: Provided that the Commissioner of Food Safety may, for reasons to be recorded in writing, approve prosecution within an extended period of up to three years.\" 6."
  },
